\section{First switch-on}

Turn the calculator on with \key{EXIT}, which doubles as the ON key. The desktop simulator opens to
the same screen. Loading the firmware onto real hardware is covered in Application Note~0
\cite{AN0000}; here we assume the calculator is already running C47 or R47.

The display has three parts. The top line is the status bar. On a new calculator it carries the date,
a right-angle mark for rectangular complex mode, a degree sign for the angle unit, the largest
fraction denominator, the integer word size and base, and a small f or g when a shift is pending.
Nearly every one of those is switchable, so yours may show more or fewer of them. The middle shows the
stack, where your numbers live. The bottom line, when a menu is open, shows the six soft-key labels;
with no menu open it shows MyMenu, which we come back to at the end.

\shot{01-home}{A new calculator, straight out of a reset: an empty stack and MyMenu along the bottom.}

Two keys rescue you at any time. \key{EXIT} backs out of a menu or an entry, and the backspace key,
\key{$\leftarrow$}, rubs out the last digit; when you are not typing it clears the number in the
display. If you feel lost, press \key{EXIT} a few times to get back to the plain stack.

\section{The RPN way}

The C47 and R47 use RPN, Reverse Polish Notation. There is no equals key. You put numbers on a small
stack of registers and then press an operation, which acts on the numbers already there. It feels
unusual for about ten minutes and then feels faster than anything else.

The stack holds eight numbers out of the box, and the bottom four, named \textsf{X}, \textsf{Y},
\textsf{Z}, and \textsf{T} from the bottom up, do nearly all the work. \textsf{X} is the number in
the display. The \key{ENTER} key pushes a copy of
\textsf{X} up into \textsf{Y} so you can type a second number without the two running together. To
add three and four: type \key{3}, press \key{ENTER} to lock it in, type \key{4}, then press \key{+}.

\seq{\key{3}\quad\key{ENTER}\quad\key{4}\quad\key{+}}{X:\ \ 7}

\shot{02-rpn}{Three plus four, the RPN way. The answer lands in \textsf{X}, the bottom line.}

The plus takes the two numbers and leaves their sum in \textsf{X}. You never said where the answer
should go; it just appeared. The stack shines when a calculation has several steps, because
intermediate results wait on it and are used automatically. To add 2, 3, and 4:

\seq{\key{2}\ \key{ENTER}\ \key{3}\ \key{ENTER}\ \key{4}\ \key{+}\ \key{+}}{X:\ \ 9}

The first plus makes 7 from 3 and 4; the second adds that to the 2 waiting underneath. A few more
keys finish everyday arithmetic. The change-sign key, \key{+/$-$}, flips the sign of \textsf{X}. The
square-root key works in place:

\seq{\key{2}\quad\key{$\sqrt{x}$}}{X:\ \ 1.414213562$\ldots$}

The \key{x$\rightleftarrows$y} key swaps \textsf{X} and \textsf{Y} when you enter two numbers in the
wrong order, and \fkey{LASTx} brings back the value that was in \textsf{X} before the last operation,
which rescues you after a slip. That is the whole idea: type a number, press \key{ENTER} only when
you must separate it from the next, and press operations as you go.

\section{Settings worth making first}

Almost everything is adjustable, and the defaults are sensible, so change as little or as much as you
like. The settings below are the ones most people reach for on day one. The full tour of
configurability is Application Note~28 \cite{AN0028}, and saving a bundle of settings to recall later
is Application Note~15 \cite{AN0015}. You need not save anything by hand for ordinary use: the
calculator writes its state to a backup file at power-off and restores it at power-on.

The single most useful first step is to set your region. Open the display menu with \fkey{DISP} and
page to the third page, where you will find \mkey{CHINA}, \mkey{EUROP}, \mkey{INDIA}, \mkey{JAPAN},
\mkey{UK}, and \mkey{USA}; the shifted row above them holds \mkey{DEFLT}, the factory choice. The menu
has three pages and the paging wraps, so a single press of the down-arrow, \key{$\downarrow$}, is the
quickest way there. One press of a region key retunes the regional conventions together, the decimal
mark and the digit grouping among them; Application Note~28 \cite{AN0028} lists everything it touches
and why the conventions differ.

\shot{03-region}{The third page of \fkey{DISP}: one press retunes the regional conventions together.}

Next, choose how many digits you see. Still under \fkey{DISP}, the first row offers \mkey{FIX},
\mkey{SCI}, \mkey{ENG}, \mkey{UNIT}, \mkey{SIG}, and \mkey{ALL}. \textsf{FIX} shows a fixed number of
decimals: press \mkey{FIX} then a digit, so \mkey{FIX}\,\key{4} shows four places and displays $\pi$
as \texttt{3.141 6}, the space being the digit grouping you chose above.
\textsf{SCI} is scientific notation, \textsf{ENG} is engineering notation in powers of a thousand, and
\textsf{ALL} shows every significant figure.

Pick your angle unit before any trigonometry. Open the mode menu with \fkey{MODE} and choose
\mkey{DEG}, \mkey{RAD}, or \mkey{GRAD}; the small circle beside each label is filled in on the one in
force. It matters at once: in degrees, the sine of 30 is exactly one half.

\shot{04-mode}{The first page of \fkey{MODE}, with \mkey{DEG} in force. A label with a line struck
through it, like \textsf{DMCP} and \textsf{ActUSB} here, is a function the build you are running does
not offer; these two need real hardware, so the simulator crosses them out.} Degrees-minutes-seconds and multiples of $\pi$ live on the trig menu, \gkey{TRG}. The date and
time formats are on the second page of the clock menu, \gkey{CLK}: page to it and pick \mkey{DMY},
\mkey{MDY}, or \mkey{YMD}, and \mkey{CLK24} or \mkey{CLK12}. Two more that some people like: the
second page of \fkey{MODE} switches between the eight-level stack you start with, \mkey{SSIZE8}, and
the classic four-level stack, \mkey{SSIZE4}, and if you work in electronics and prefer $j$ for the
imaginary unit, the second page of \fkey{DISP} offers \mkey{CPXi} and \mkey{CPXj}.

\section{Finding your way around the menus}

There are more functions than keys, so most functions live in menus. A menu fills the bottom of the
screen with up to six labels, one above each soft key; press the soft key to run it. Many menus run
to several pages, and the up and down arrows page through them. Each label can carry a shifted
function too, shown on the lines above it and reached with a shift before the soft key.

When you cannot remember where something is, the catalog finds it. \fkey{CAT}\ opens alphabetical
catalogs of every function, menu, variable, and program: \mkey{FCNS} for functions, \mkey{CNST} for
physical constants, \mkey{CHARS} for characters, \mkey{PROGS} for your own programs, \mkey{VARS} for
your variables, and \mkey{MENUS} for the menus themselves. Pick a group and read down the list.
Selecting an item runs it, or, while you are typing a program, inserts it.

\shot{06-catalog}{\fkey{CAT} and its six groups. Everything the calculator can do is in here somewhere.} The complete keyboard, key
by key, is mapped on pages~\pageref{fig:ckbd} and~\pageref{fig:rkbd}, and Application Note~18
\cite{AN0018} covers it in detail.

\section{A short tour}

The next few pages show things a plain calculator cannot do. Try them in order.

\subsection{Complex numbers}

The calculator treats complex numbers as ordinary values. A switch called \textsf{CPXRES} decides
whether a real function that would leave the real line returns a complex number instead of an error.
It is already on when the calculator is new; if you ever find it turned off, put it back with
\fkey{MODE}, second page, \mkey{CPXRES}. The quickest way to see which way the switch is set is simply
to use it. Take the square root of minus one:

\seq{\key{1}\quad\key{+/$-$}\quad\key{$\sqrt{x}$}}{X:\ \ 1.i}

That is $i$, the imaginary unit, which no real-only calculator will give you; a pure imaginary is
shown as \texttt{1.i}, with no real part written. To type a complex
number directly, enter the real part, press the \key{i} operator (the blue function on the sine key),
and enter the imaginary part. Multiplying $(1+2i)$ by $(3+4i)$:

\seq{\key{1}\ \key{i}\ \key{2}\quad\key{ENTER}\quad\key{3}\ \key{i}\ \key{4}\quad\key{$\times$}}{X:\ \ -5.+10.i}

\shot{05-cplx}{$(1+2i)(3+4i)$ worked out on the stack. Complex values sit in the registers exactly as
real ones do.}

which is right, since $(1+2i)(3+4i) = 3 + 10i + 8i^{2} = -5 + 10i$. There is also a \fkey{COMPLEX} key
that joins two stack numbers into one complex value, and a whole \gkey{CPX} menu of complex tools.
Complex numbers, rectangular and polar, are the subject of Application Notes~1 and~10
\cite{AN0001, AN0010}.

\subsection{The equation solver}

The solver finds the value of a variable that makes an equation true, that is, it finds roots. You
give the calculator your function as a short program, hand the solver two guesses, and it hunts down
the answer. Let us solve $x^{2} + 2x - 2 = 0$.

First write the function as a program named FUNC. Declaring the variable with \mkey{MVAR} is what lets
the solver later ask you for it, and lets the grapher plot it.

\prog{0001: LBL 'FUNC'\\0002: \ \ MVAR 'x'\\0003: \ \ RCL 'x'\\0004: \ \ ENTER\\0005: \ \ x²\\0006: \ \ x⇄y\\0007: \ \ 2\\0008: \ \ ×\\0009: \ \ +\\0010: \ \ 2\\0011: \ \ −\\0012: END}

To key it in, open a new program with \key{XEQ}\,\key{.}\,\key{.} and switch to program-entry mode with
\fkey{PRGM}. Start the label with \gkey{LBL}, then \fkey{$\alpha$}, type \key{F}\,\key{U}\,\key{N}\,\key{C},
and \key{ENTER}. Declare the variable next. MVAR lives two pages into the program-functions menu that
is now on the screen: press \mkey{P.FN1}, then \mkey{P.FN2}, then \mkey{MVAR}, and type
\fkey{$\alpha$}\,\key{x}\,\key{ENTER}. Now key the function: \key{RCL}\,\fkey{$\alpha$}\,\key{x}\,\key{ENTER}, then
\key{ENTER}, \fkey{$x^{2}$}, \key{x$\rightleftarrows$y}, \key{2}, \key{$\times$}, \key{+}, \key{2},
\key{$-$}. Leave program-entry mode with \fkey{PRGM}.

While you are still in program-entry mode the screen lists what you have typed, so you can check it
against the listing above before you leave. The up and down arrows walk the listing.

\shot{07-func}{FUNC keyed in, seen from the top. The header line counts the bytes and the steps.}

Now solve it. Open \fkey{ADV}, choose \mkey{SOLVE}, and name the program with
\fkey{$\alpha$}\,\key{F}\,\key{U}\,\key{N}\,\key{C}\,\key{ENTER}. The variable \textsf{x} now appears as
a menu key, and it is the only one, because FUNC declared only that one \mkey{MVAR}.

\shot{08-mvar}{After naming the program, its variable appears as a soft key. This one menu key does
all three jobs: it takes the first guess, the second, and then the solve.}

Give a guess below the root and one above it, pressing the \textsf{x} key after each, then
press it once more, with nothing typed ahead of it, to solve:

\seq{\key{2}\,\key{+/$-$}\ \ \mkey{x}\ \ \key{2}\ \ \mkey{x}\ \ \mkey{x}}{x:\ \ 0.732050807568877$\ldots$}

\shot{09-solve}{The root, found. The star on the menu key marks the variable that was solved for.}

which is $-1+\sqrt{3}$, one of the two roots. Guide it toward the other with more negative guesses, say
$-11$ and $-10$, and it returns $-2.732050807568877\ldots$, which is $-1-\sqrt{3}$. Each solve returns one root;
you steer it to the next by changing the guesses, exactly as on the classic HP machines. Full worked
examples, including the algebraic \fkey{EQN} route where you type the formula as on paper, are in
Application Notes~11 and~22 \cite{AN0011, AN0022}.

\subsection{Graphing a function}

The calculator draws graphs on its own screen, and it is worth seeing it do something beautiful. Let
us plot a wave packet, $f(x) = \sin(5x)\,e^{-x^{2}/8}$: a burst of oscillation held inside a smooth
bell-shaped envelope, the shape of a pulse of light or a plucked string fading to silence. It is a
handful of keystrokes and it shows off the screen.

Switch to radians first with \fkey{MODE}, \mkey{RAD}. Then enter the function as a program, the same
way you built FUNC:

\prog{0001: LBL 'WAVE'\\0002: \ \ MVAR 'x'\\0003: \ \ RCL 'x'\\0004: \ \ 5\\0005: \ \ ×\\0006: \ \ SIN\\0007: \ \ RCL 'x'\\0008: \ \ x²\\0009: \ \ 8\\0010: \ \ ÷\\0011: \ \ CHS\\0012: \ \ e\textasciicircum x\\0013: \ \ ×\\0014: END}

To key it in, open a new program with \key{XEQ}\,\key{.}\,\key{.}, \fkey{PRGM}, then \gkey{LBL}
\fkey{$\alpha$}\,\key{W}\,\key{A}\,\key{V}\,\key{E}\,\key{ENTER}, and declare the variable as before:
\mkey{P.FN1}, \mkey{P.FN2}, \mkey{MVAR}, then \fkey{$\alpha$}\,\key{x}\,\key{ENTER}. Then key the
function: \key{RCL}\,\fkey{$\alpha$}\,\key{x}\,\key{ENTER}, \key{5}, \key{$\times$}, \key{SIN}, then
\key{RCL}\,\fkey{$\alpha$}\,\key{x}\,\key{ENTER}, \fkey{$x^{2}$}, \key{8}, \key{$\div$}, \key{+/$-$},
\fkey{$e^{x}$}, \key{$\times$}. Leave with \fkey{PRGM}. Now plot it: \fkey{ADV}, \mkey{PLT\,f}, name it
\fkey{$\alpha$}\,\key{W}\,\key{A}\,\key{V}\,\key{E}\,\key{ENTER}, and the variable \textsf{x} appears
as a menu key, just as it did for the solver. Give it the two ends of the range, pressing the
\textsf{x} key after each, then press it once more to draw:

\seq{\key{5}\,\key{+/$-$}\ \ \mkey{x}\ \ \key{5}\ \ \mkey{x}\ \ \mkey{x}}{plots WAVE, $-5 \le x \le 5$}

The screen fills with a train of waves swelling to a peak in the middle and dying away smoothly to
each side, with a zoom menu below. Press \key{EXIT} when you are done looking.

\shot{10-wave}{$\sin(5x)\,e^{-x^{2}/8}$ over $-5 \le x \le 5$. The corners give the range covered, the
tick spacing, and where the axes cross. The menu underneath zooms in and out, forces a square aspect,
and resets the plot.} Change the \key{5} to
a larger number for faster wiggles, or the \key{8} for a wider envelope, and plot again. Plotting, including scatter plots of data and curves in the complex plane,
is covered in Application Notes~3, 13, and~22 \cite{AN0003, AN0013, AN0022}.

\subsection{Fitting a line to data}

The calculator is a quietly powerful statistics machine. Suppose you measured a quantity $y$ at five
settings of $x$ and recorded the pairs $(1,3)$, $(2,5)$, $(3,7)$, $(4,9)$, $(5,11)$. Enter each pair
with the \key{$\Sigma$+} key at the top left: key the $y$ value, \key{ENTER}, the $x$ value, then
\key{$\Sigma$+}.

\seq{\key{3}\,\key{ENTER}\,\key{1}\,\key{$\Sigma$+}\ \ \ \key{5}\,\key{ENTER}\,\key{2}\,\key{$\Sigma$+}\ \ \ \dots\ \ \ \key{11}\,\key{ENTER}\,\key{5}\,\key{$\Sigma$+}}{5 points}

Now open the statistics menu, \fkey{STAT}, and read your data off it. \mkey{$\bar{x}$} gives the mean
of the $x$ values, $3$, with the mean of the $y$ values just above in \textsf{Y}, and \mkey{s} gives
the sample standard deviation, $1.5811$; the \mkey{$\sigma$} key beside it gives the population
version, $1.4142$.

\shot{11-stat}{\fkey{STAT} with the five pairs entered. The last four $y$ values are still on the
stack behind the menu.}

Open the regression submenu: press the shift key twice, then the \mkey{REGR} soft
key. There \mkey{r} reports the correlation, $1$: the points lie on a perfect straight line. Its
intercept and slope sit on the blue row of the same menu, so press shift twice before each:
\mkey{LR:a0} is $1$ and \mkey{LR:a1} is $2$, so the line is
$y = 2x + 1$. Best of all, you can predict. Key a new $x$ and press \mkey{$\hat{y}$}; at $x = 6$ the
fitted line gives

\seq{\key{6}\ \ \mkey{$\hat{y}$}}{y:\ \ 13}

\shot{12-yhat}{The regression menu, and the line's prediction at $x = 6$.}

Real data is rarely this tidy, and that is where the rest of the menu earns its keep: the correlation
tells you how well a line fits, and a \mkey{MODEL} choice fits curves, logarithms, exponentials, and
powers just as easily. Curve fitting and statistical plots are covered in Application Notes~3 and~13
\cite{AN0003, AN0013}.

\subsection{Writing a small program}

A program is a recorded sequence of keystrokes with a name, and writing one is just doing the
calculation by hand in program-entry mode. Let us make a program \textsf{S} that squares \textsf{X}.
Open a fresh program with \key{XEQ}\,\key{.}\,\key{.} as before, press \fkey{PRGM} to enter
program-entry mode, key the label and the one step that does the work, then press \fkey{PRGM} again
to leave. The \gkey{LBL} key starts a label, the \fkey{$\alpha$} key switches to letter entry so you
can type the name, and \key{ENTER} finishes the name.

\seq{\key{XEQ}\,\key{.}\,\key{.}\quad\fkey{PRGM}\quad\gkey{LBL}\ \fkey{$\alpha$}\ \key{S}\ \key{ENTER}\ \fkey{$x^{2}$}\ \fkey{PRGM}}{0001: LBL 'S'}

\shot{13-prgm}{The whole program: a name, one step of work, and the \textsf{END} the calculator adds
for you.}

Now put a number in \textsf{X} and run the program by name:

\seq{\key{5}\quad\key{XEQ}\ \fkey{$\alpha$}\ \key{S}\ \key{ENTER}}{X:\ \ 25}

The program squared 5 to give 25. Anything you can do by hand you can record this way and replay it,
or assign it to a key so it runs with one press. Programs can also loop and decide, which turns the
calculator into a small computer: the \mkey{$\Sigma$n} function on the \fkey{ADV} menu runs a labeled
program once per term and adds the results, so summing the squares from 1 to 3 gives $1 + 4 + 9 = 14$.
Programmed sums are the subject of Application Notes~8 and~9 \cite{AN0008, AN0009}.

\section{Saving and restoring your work}

Your work is safe without any effort on your part. When you switch the calculator off it writes its
whole state, the stack, your variables, your programs, and every setting, to a backup file, and reads
it all back when you switch on. You never lose your place, even across a battery change.

You can also take a named snapshot to keep, which is worth doing before a big experiment. Open the
input and output menu with \gkey{I/O} and press \mkey{SAVEST}; the calculator opens its file screen,
where you give the snapshot a name such as MYWORK and confirm with \key{ENTER}. The whole state is
written to a file of that name. Now tinker as freely as you like; when you want it all back, open
\gkey{I/O} again, press the shift key once and then \mkey{LOADST}, which sits on the orange row just
above \mkey{SAVEST}, and choose your file. Everything returns exactly as it was, down to the number in
\textsf{X}.

\shot{14-io}{The \gkey{I/O} menu. \mkey{SAVEST} writes a state file and \mkey{LOADST}, directly above
it on the orange row, reads one back.}

\section{Making the calculator your own}

You can gather the functions and programs you use most into a menu of your own, called MyMenu, so they
are always a press away instead of buried in a catalog. You have seen MyMenu already: it is the menu
sitting at the bottom of the screen whenever nothing else is open, stocked from the factory with six
starters, DRG, $y^{x}$, $x^{2}$, $10^{x}$, $e^{x}$, and the polar $i$. Press \key{EXIT} until the
other menus close and it reappears. Those starters are yours to overwrite.

Say you want your squaring program \textsf{S} and the sine function on it. Press \fkey{ASN}, which is
the yellow shift then the \key{1} key, to begin an assignment. Choose what to assign: for the program,
open the catalog with \fkey{CAT}, choose \mkey{PROGS}, and pick \mkey{S}; for a plain function like
\key{SIN}, just press its key. Then press \fkey{USER}, which folds the menus away and brings MyMenu
back, and press the soft key where you want the item to sit. Repeat for each one. From then on, those
soft keys run your chosen items.

A long press of the yellow shift key opens the HOME menu of common functions; on the third page of
\fkey{MODE} you can point that long press at MyMenu instead, and choose which of the two greets you as
the resting menu. The same \fkey{ASN} command assigns anything, a function, a
program, a variable, or a whole menu, to any key you like, so you can shape the entire keyboard around
your work. Assignments, the four editable menus, and the ready-made ribbons of common functions are
covered in Application Note~18 \cite{AN0018}.

\section{Where to go next}

You now have the essentials: RPN entry, the settings that make the calculator yours, the menu system,
and a first look at complex numbers, solving, graphing, and programming. Application Note~18
\cite{AN0018} is the full keyboard reference, Application Note~28 \cite{AN0028} tours everything you
can configure, and if you come from an HP-42S, Application Note~26 \cite{AN0026} explains what is
compatible and what is new. The complete function reference lives at 47calc.com \cite{Ref47},
alongside the project wikis \cite{Wiki1, Wiki2}. Most of all, experiment: the backup keeps your state
safe, \key{EXIT} always backs you out, and nothing you press survives a fresh start.

